A minimal example of modifying cpufreq to use PowerOP for reading and
writing power parameters on OMAP1710.

#ifdef approach is introduced to simplify comparision of original and updated
implementations. ifdefs are expected to be removed in the future.

cpufreq utilizes "cpu" power parameter only.  The values for other power
parameters are presented in the opoints array for reference as an example
of how cpufreq may be extended to handle wider range of power
parameters. One can think of all power parameters except
"cpu" in opoints array as it set to -1:

struct powerop_point opoints[] = {
  { -1, -1, 192000, -1 -1 -1 -1 -1 -1},
  { -1, -1 , 96000, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1},
  { -1, -1,  48000, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1},
  { -1, -1,  24000, -1, -1, -1, -1, -1},
};

where -1 means that cpufreq does not care about anyhing but cpu frequency.

The are two patches attached. The patch order is :

 handle.mpu.clock.reference.count.correctly.fix.patch
 powerop.support.for.omap1.cpufreq.driver.patch

handle.mpu.clock.reference.count.correctly.fix.patch is just trivial fix for
minor issue in common code for both implementations.

